--TEST--
Outside a `#[Test]` method a failed assertion is an ordinary throw: nothing catches it, the program ends at status 1 with standard output intact, and the floor's record names `Core\Test\Failure` — `rule:testing/failure-ledger`, `rule:errors/engine-floor`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:errors/assertion-failures
// The runner is what turns a failure into a reported row, and a program run
// from the command line has no runner. What is left is `rule:errors/propagation`
// and nothing else, which is the half of this feature a script that checks its
// own preconditions depends on.

array<string> $config = ["host" => "localhost", "port" => "5432"];

// The assertion that holds returns, so the line after it runs and the line is
// kept: an ending does not take back what was already written.
Core\Test::assertCount($config, 2);
echo "the configuration has both keys\n";

// This one throws, with no `#[Test]` method above it to catch it and no arm
// around it, so the program ends here at status 1.
Core\Test::assertTrue(Core\Arr::hasKey($config, "password"),
    {message: "the password is missing"});

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--
the configuration has both keys
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
{"level":"error","msg":"the password is missing: Core\\Test::assertTrue failed: `$actual` is false","fields":{"class":"Core\\Test\\Failure"},"nodes":[{"function":"<script>","file":"%s","line":%d}]}
